**Accepted queued design.** `$` introduces a registered compiler or builder directive. Template directives such as `$md` and `$code` are implemented. General non-template directives are accepted design and are not yet available in the Alpha compiler. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.

A `$` marker is not automatically harmless metadata. Compiler-owned directives may impose real contracts. Builder extensions cannot rewrite functions or weaken language safety.

### Current versus accepted syntax

Today, `config.moth` uses declaration-owned `#Config of T` inputs with grouped
parenthesised `project` and `html` records. Direct const root inserts supply
HTML metadata through builder slots. The accepted configuration shape is:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $project(
        name = "my_site",
    )

    $html_builder
]

Version is optional, and there is no `entry_root`; the selected directory is the source root.

General `$entry` root-purpose validation remains queued. It does not replace
builder slots. See @../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata).

### Directive argument basics

- Supply arguments with `$name(arguments)`.
- Supply none with `$name`.
- Authored empty `()` is invalid for directives.
- Ordinary function calls still use `f()`.

`$html_builder` and `$page` may be written with no parentheses when every parameter has a default. `$html_builder()` and `$page()` are invalid.

Exact signatures live in @../project-structure/#project-config (Project config) and @../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and page directives).
